Supersedes the fixes on ac556dd and 191771a, not just building on them. Those two commits optimized a hand-rolled join (build a noted-commit set via `git notes list`, then match/sort it in bash) after the previous round found it was forking git once per commit. This commit removes the hand-rolled join entirely: `git log`'s own `%N` format placeholder already returns each commit's note (empty string if none) in the same process that walks history, pre-sorted newest-first, for free. Flagged in review as "you optimized the wrong layer" -- the fix for the O(N) fork bug should have been "stop reimplementing what git log already does," not "reimplement it more carefully."

Before switching to it, verified %N follows `core.notesRef` the same way `git notes show` does (tested by setting a non-default core.notesRef in a scratch repo and confirming both commands picked up the same custom ref automatically, no extra `notes.displayRef` config needed) -- otherwise this rewrite could have silently diverged from `git notes show`/`git notes list` behavior for anyone not using the default notes ref.

Mechanics: each script now runs one `git log --format="%H<US>%s<US>%N<RS>"` call (US = \x1f, RS = \x1e -- rare control bytes chosen so they can't collide with real commit text), parsed with `read -r -d "$RS"` per record and `${var%%"$US"*}`-style splits per field. %N always appends exactly one trailing newline when a note exists and produces zero bytes when it doesn't (confirmed with `od -c` before relying on it) -- each script strips that one trailing newline so printed note bodies match byte-for-byte what the old `git notes show`/`git show <blob>` output produced.

This eliminates, across all three scripts: the `git notes list` call, all per-match `git show`/`git notes show` fetches, the parallel match_ts/match_sha/match_note arrays and manual sort-by-timestamp in git-notes-grep.sh (git log's natural order was already correct), the noted-set membership test in git-why-record.sh, and the nested notes_commit_arr/notes_blob_arr linear-scan in git-why.sh. Re-verified every scenario from the prior two rounds (metachar keywords, no-notes-fetched clone, path scoping, LICENSE-only-touched-by-unnoted-commit) produces byte-identical output to before.
